 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L11_001_A Point Class",
   "display_name": "A Point Class",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In this lab we build a small <b>drawing program</b> out of classes, step by step: a <code>Point</code>, then a <code>Line</code> made of two points, a <code>Triangle</code> made of three points, and a <code>Scene</code> holding many shapes. You will write small methods that <b>move, flip, turn and scale</b> these shapes, and you can <b>see</b> what your methods do.</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Point</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x, y, color</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">move()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">flip_x()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">rotate90()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">...</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Line</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">p1, p2 (Points)</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">move()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">...</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Triangle</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">a, b, c (Points)</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">move()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">...</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Scene</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">shapes (a list)</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">move_all()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">...</div></div></div></div><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">Line, Triangle and Scene are made of other objects</div>\n<p>In every question, two methods are <b>given</b> (do not change them):</p>\n<ul><li><code>__str__</code>, so that <code>print(p)</code> shows the point as <code>(x, y)</code>;</li><li><code>draw(ax)</code>, which draws the shape with <b>matplotlib</b>, Python's plotting library. You do not need to understand it in detail: <code>ax</code> is the drawing area, and <code>ax.scatter([x], [y], color=...)</code> puts a dot at <code>(x, y)</code>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Seeing your work:</b> at the bottom of the answer box there is a block of drawing code with <code>#</code> in front of every line. Remove the <code>#</code> signs and press <b>Pre-check</b>: a picture appears under the results, with the shape <b>before</b> the change in grey and <b>after</b> it in colour. (The first time, loading the drawing library takes a few seconds.) The drawing is not graded; the tests only check the coordinates.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the constructor <code>__init__</code> of the class <code>Point</code>. It takes <code>x</code>, <code>y</code> and an optional <code>color</code> (default <code>\"black\"</code>), and stores them as the attributes <code>x</code>, <code>y</code> and <code>color</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>p = Point(3, 4, \"red\")</code>, <code>print(p)</code> prints <code>(3, 4)</code> and <code>p.color</code> is <code>\"red\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        # write your code here\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=color or self.color, zorder=3)\n\n\n# ---- See it! (not graded) Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check ----\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# ax.axhline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# ax.axvline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# p = Point(3, 4, \"red\")\n# p.draw(ax)\n# Point(-2, 1).draw(ax)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
