  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L11_002_Moving A Point",
   "display_name": "Moving A Point",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <b>method</b> can change the object's attributes. Moving a point by <code>dx</code> to the right and <code>dy</code> up means adding <code>dx</code> to <code>x</code> and <code>dy</code> to <code>y</code>:</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">p : Point</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x = 2</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">y = 1</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;padding:22px 8px 0 0;color:#8a939c\">p.move(3, 2) &rarr;</div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">p : Point</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x = 5</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">y = 3</div></div></div></div><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">the same object, with new coordinates</div>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the method <code>move(dx, dy)</code>, which moves the point. It changes the point and returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>p = Point(2, 1)</code> and <code>p.move(3, 2)</code>, <code>print(p)</code> prints <code>(5, 3)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=color or self.color, zorder=3)\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- See it! (not graded) Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check ----\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# ax.axhline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# ax.axvline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# Point(2, 1).draw(ax, \"grey\")      # before\n# p = Point(2, 1, \"red\")\n# p.move(3, 2)\n# p.draw(ax)                      # after\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "p = Point(2, 1)\np.move(3, 2)\nprint(p)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "(5, 3)",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
