  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L11_004_A Quarter Turn",
   "display_name": "A Quarter Turn",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Turning a point a <b>quarter turn</b> (90 degrees) <b>anticlockwise</b> around the origin <code>(0, 0)</code> has a simple rule:</p>\n<pre>(x, y)  becomes  (-y, x)</pre>\n<p>For example, <code>(3, 1)</code> becomes <code>(-1, 3)</code>; turning again gives <code>(-3, -1)</code>, then <code>(1, -3)</code>, and a fourth turn brings it back to <code>(3, 1)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Careful:</b> the new <code>x</code> uses the old <code>y</code>, and the new <code>y</code> uses the old <code>x</code>. If you change <code>self.x</code> first, the old value is gone. Either save the old values in variables first, or assign both at once: <code>self.x, self.y = -self.y, self.x</code> (the right side is computed first, then both are stored).</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the method <code>rotate90()</code>, which turns the point a quarter turn anticlockwise around the origin.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>p = Point(3, 1)</code> and <code>p.rotate90()</code>, <code>print(p)</code> prints <code>(-1, 3)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=color or self.color, zorder=3)\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n\n    def flip_x(self):\n        self.y = -self.y        # mirror in the x-axis\n\n    def flip_y(self):\n        self.x = -self.x        # mirror in the y-axis\n\n    def rotate90(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- See it! (not graded) Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check ----\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# ax.axhline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# ax.axvline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# p = Point(3, 1, \"red\")\n# for i in range(4):\n#     p.draw(ax)\n#     p.rotate90()\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
