  {
   "xml_index": 8,
   "xml_name": "L11_008_A Line Made Of Points",
   "display_name": "A Line Made Of Points",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <code>Line</code> is defined by its two end points, so its attributes are <b>two <code>Point</code> objects</b>. An object built from other objects is called <b>composition</b>. The line can use the points' own methods: its length is simply <code>self.p1.distance_to(self.p2)</code>.</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">line : Line</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">p1 = &#x27;Point(0, 0)&#x27;</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">p2 = &#x27;Point(3, 4)&#x27;</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;padding:22px 8px 0 0;color:#8a939c\">&rarr;</div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">p1 : Point</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x = 0</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">y = 0</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">p2 : Point</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">x = 3</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">y = 4</div></div></div></div>\n<p>The complete <code>Point</code> class is given. In <code>Line</code>, <code>__str__</code> and <code>draw</code> are given.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the constructor of <code>Line</code> (taking <code>p1</code>, <code>p2</code> and an optional <code>color</code>, default <code>\"blue\"</code>, stored as attributes with the same names) and the method <code>length()</code>, which returns the distance between the two points.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4)).length()</code> returns <code>5.0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=color or self.color, zorder=3)\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n\n    def flip_x(self):\n        self.y = -self.y        # mirror in the x-axis\n\n    def flip_y(self):\n        self.x = -self.x        # mirror in the y-axis\n\n    def rotate90(self):\n        # a quarter turn anticlockwise about the origin: (x, y) -> (-y, x)\n        self.x, self.y = -self.y, self.x\n\n    def scale(self, k):\n        self.x = self.x * k\n        self.y = self.y * k\n\n    def rotate90_about(self, cx, cy):\n        # move the centre to the origin, turn, and move back: three existing methods\n        self.move(-cx, -cy)\n        self.rotate90()\n        self.move(cx, cy)\n\n    def distance_to(self, other):\n        return round(((other.x - self.x) ** 2 + (other.y - self.y) ** 2) ** 0.5, 2)\n\nclass Line:\n    def __init__(self, p1, p2, color=\"blue\"):\n        # write your code here\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return str(self.p1) + \"-\" + str(self.p2)\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.plot([self.p1.x, self.p2.x], [self.p1.y, self.p2.y], color=color or self.color)\n\n    def length(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- See it! (not graded) Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check ----\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# ax.axhline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# ax.axvline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4)).draw(ax)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "line = Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4))\nprint(line, line.length())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "(0, 0)-(3, 4) 5.0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "line = Line(Point(1, 1), Point(4, 1), \"red\")\nprint(line.length(), line.color)",
