   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L11_009_Moving A Line",
   "display_name": "Moving A Line",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Moving a line means moving <b>both of its points</b>, and each point already knows how to move. So the line simply asks its points to do it: <code>self.p1.move(dx, dy)</code> and <code>self.p2.move(dx, dy)</code>. Passing work on to the objects you are made of is called <b>delegation</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the method <code>move(dx, dy)</code> of <code>Line</code>, by calling <code>move</code> on both points.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>line = Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4))</code> and <code>line.move(1, 2)</code>, <code>print(line)</code> prints <code>(1, 2)-(4, 6)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=color or self.color, zorder=3)\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n\n    def flip_x(self):\n        self.y = -self.y        # mirror in the x-axis\n\n    def flip_y(self):\n        self.x = -self.x        # mirror in the y-axis\n\n    def rotate90(self):\n        # a quarter turn anticlockwise about the origin: (x, y) -> (-y, x)\n        self.x, self.y = -self.y, self.x\n\n    def scale(self, k):\n        self.x = self.x * k\n        self.y = self.y * k\n\n    def rotate90_about(self, cx, cy):\n        # move the centre to the origin, turn, and move back: three existing methods\n        self.move(-cx, -cy)\n        self.rotate90()\n        self.move(cx, cy)\n\n    def distance_to(self, other):\n        return round(((other.x - self.x) ** 2 + (other.y - self.y) ** 2) ** 0.5, 2)\n\nclass Line:\n    def __init__(self, p1, p2, color=\"blue\"):\n        self.p1 = p1            # two Point objects\n        self.p2 = p2\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return str(self.p1) + \"-\" + str(self.p2)\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.plot([self.p1.x, self.p2.x], [self.p1.y, self.p2.y], color=color or self.color)\n\n    def length(self):\n        return self.p1.distance_to(self.p2)     # ask a point to do the work\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- See it! (not graded) Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check ----\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# ax.axhline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# ax.axvline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4)).draw(ax, 'grey')\n# line = Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4), \"red\")\n# line.move(1, 2)\n# line.draw(ax)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "line = Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4))\nline.move(1, 2)\nprint(line)",
