  {
   "xml_index": 14,
   "xml_name": "L11_014_Copying A Triangle",
   "display_name": "Copying A Triangle",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>To draw a pattern, we often want a <b>copy</b> of a shape that we can move without changing the original. Remember <b>aliasing</b> from Lab 7: <code>t2 = t</code> does not copy anything; both names refer to the same triangle, so moving <code>t2</code> would move <code>t</code> too.</p>\n<p>A real copy must be a <b>new <code>Triangle</code></b> with <b>new <code>Point</code> objects</b> at the same coordinates (if the copy shared the original's points, moving the copy would still move the original's corners).</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the method <code>copy()</code> of <code>Triangle</code>, which returns a new triangle with new points at the same coordinates and the same colour.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>t = Triangle(Point(1, 1), Point(4, 1), Point(1, 3))</code>, <code>c = t.copy()</code> and <code>c.move(5, 0)</code>, <code>print(t)</code> still prints <code>(1, 1) (4, 1) (1, 3)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, color=\"black\"):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return \"(\" + str(self.x) + \", \" + str(self.y) + \")\"\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        ax.scatter([self.x], [self.y], color=color or self.color, zorder=3)\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n\n    def flip_x(self):\n        self.y = -self.y        # mirror in the x-axis\n\n    def flip_y(self):\n        self.x = -self.x        # mirror in the y-axis\n\n    def rotate90(self):\n        # a quarter turn anticlockwise about the origin: (x, y) -> (-y, x)\n        self.x, self.y = -self.y, self.x\n\n    def scale(self, k):\n        self.x = self.x * k\n        self.y = self.y * k\n\n    def rotate90_about(self, cx, cy):\n        # move the centre to the origin, turn, and move back: three existing methods\n        self.move(-cx, -cy)\n        self.rotate90()\n        self.move(cx, cy)\n\n    def distance_to(self, other):\n        return round(((other.x - self.x) ** 2 + (other.y - self.y) ** 2) ** 0.5, 2)\n\nclass Triangle:\n    def __init__(self, a, b, c, color=\"orange\"):\n        self.a = a              # three Point objects (the corners)\n        self.b = b\n        self.c = c\n        self.color = color\n\n    # ---- given: printing and drawing (do not change) ----\n    def __str__(self):\n        return str(self.a) + \" \" + str(self.b) + \" \" + str(self.c)\n\n    def draw(self, ax, color=None):\n        xs = [self.a.x, self.b.x, self.c.x]\n        ys = [self.a.y, self.b.y, self.c.y]\n        ax.fill(xs, ys, color=color or self.color, alpha=0.5)\n\n    def corners(self):\n        return [self.a, self.b, self.c]\n\n    def move(self, dx, dy):\n        for p in self.corners():        # move every corner\n            p.move(dx, dy)\n\n    def flip_x(self):\n        for p in self.corners():\n            p.flip_x()\n\n    def flip_y(self):\n        for p in self.corners():\n            p.flip_y()\n\n    def scale(self, k):\n        for p in self.corners():\n            p.scale(k)\n\n    def rotate90(self):\n        for p in self.corners():\n            p.rotate90()\n\n    def copy(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- See it! (not graded) Remove the # in front of the lines below, then press Pre-check ----\n# import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n# fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n# ax.set_aspect('equal')\n# ax.axhline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# ax.axvline(0, color='lightgrey')\n# t = Triangle(Point(1, 1), Point(4, 1), Point(1, 3))\n# c = t.copy()\n# c.move(5, 0)\n# t.draw(ax, 'grey')\n# c.draw(ax)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
